device helpers the stopped runner left running that `runner stop` then ended
(`kind`, `pid`, `processGroup`, `command`, and `killed`, true once it was
signalled; on iOS, the `simctl diagnose` that
WebDriverAgent's xcodebuild starts when it is interrupted), and is `[]` when
there were none. Only processes the runner started are ended: they name the
device and descend from the runner, or write into its run directory under the
runner home. `bundle_id`, and
`udid` on Android, are the 2.1 names, kept for one release and deprecated.

`.grantiva/session.json`, the file `runner start` leaves for `runner stop`,
`dump-hierarchy` and the MCP server, uses the same names as `runner start
--json`: `pid`, `port`, `platform`, `bundleId` and `udid` on iOS,
`applicationId`, `serial` and `udid` on Android, plus `startedAt` and `state`.
It is written the moment the runner is spawned with `"state": "starting"` and
no port, and rewritten with `"state": "ready"` once the driver answers; what
commands say meanwhile is in [docs/mcp.md](docs/mcp.md#a-session-that-is-starting).
grantiva 2.1.0 reads a `starting` file (no port yet) as no session at
all. The 2.1 names stay beside the new ones for one release, and files
written by 2.1 are still read:

- `wdaPort` → `port` (.grantiva/session.json)
- `bundleId` → `applicationId` (.grantiva/session.json on Android)

A foreground `runner start` ended by `grantiva runner stop` exits 0. One ended
by `simulator teardown` or `emulator teardown`, run from any directory and at
any point after `Runner started` (its session flow still running or its hold
armed), exits 0 too and says `Runner stopped by teardown (pid N); the session has ended.`: the
teardown writes `<pid>.stop.json` beside the runner's owner file in
`/tmp/grantiva-sessions` before it stops it, so the marker is found whatever
the directory or runner home either side uses. A marker counts only for the
very process it names, and it is removed once read, or once its runner and
its `runner start` are both gone.

`runner stop` releases a `run --keep-alive` session started from the current
directory as Ctrl-C would, whether its flows are still running (the run ends
interrupted, exit 130; `port` is `null` in `--json` until the runner has
published its session) or finished. If that run's flows had already finished,
its `--ready-file` keeps the `passed` or `failed` it wrote (the ready file is
written once) and the run exits with the status they earned; the `stopped`
result of `runner stop` is the record of the release.

```json
{"pid": 64253, "platform": "ios", "port": 8235, "status": "started",
 "bundleId": "com.example.app", "udid": "A1C8EC2A-FB08-4EAC-A738-51E98D13CDBF"}
```

`runner install --json` reports `"extracted": false` when the installed runner
was already current (the text output then says `Runner already installed at …`).

### stdout is the result, stderr is the commentary

Everything a caller captures or parses — a `--json` payload, the `ensure` UDID,
a result table, a dumped hierarchy — goes to **stdout**, undecorated. Progress
narration, warnings, and errors go to **stderr**. So both of these work, and
neither sees the other's output:

```bash
udid=$(grantiva simulator ensure --name "iPhone 17 Pro")
grantiva doctor --json | jq '.[] | select(.status == "error")'
```

Each doctor check's `status` is `ok`, `warning` or `error`. Only `error` makes `grantiva doctor` exit non-zero;
a `warning` (an advisory check such as no booted simulator) never does.

Every `--json` document, and the `--ready-file` file, uses camelCase keys.

Some documents used snake_case keys before. For one deprecation period they
write each of those values twice, under the camelCase key and under the old
snake_case key beside it; read the camelCase key, as the snake_case one will be
removed in a later release. The keys still written in both spellings:

